verses.text:
Hungry people eat his harvest,
even taking grain from among the thorns.{b}
Thirsty ones{c} gasp for his wealth.

Lying in bed, I wonder, ‘When will I be able to get up?’
But the evening drags on,{a}
and I roll from side to side until sunrise.

You bring new witnesses to testify against me.
Your anger toward me grows.
Fresh troops of troubles attack me.{a}

a land as black as midnight,{c}
with deep shadow and no order,
where even the light is like blackness.’”

and tell you the hidden things of wisdom,
for real wisdom{b} has two sides to it.
Then you would see that God is asking for less than your guilt deserves.{c}

Robbers live in peaceful tents,
and those who anger God are safe,
the ones who hold their own god in their fists.{b}

I myself will see him,
and my own eyes will look, not those of a stranger.{i}
My heart{j} faints inside me.

then be afraid of the sword.
Fury calls down the sword’s penalty,
so that you will know there is a judgment.”{l}

While he is filling his stomach,
God{c} will send his blazing anger against him
and rain it down on him as his meal.{d}

In the city the dying groan.
The wounded cry out for help.
Yet God does not call it wrong.{d}

They cut a shaft far from where anyone lives.{b}
No foot has been near.
They dangle far from people and swing back and forth.

They tear up my road.
They help my disaster along,
and no one stops them.{h}

He sings out to others and says,
‘I sinned and twisted what was straight,
and I did not get what I deserved.{k}

“So hear me, you who have understanding.{b}
God is far above doing evil.
Shaddai is far above doing wrong.

He also wants to lead you out of the jaws of trouble
into a wide-open place with no walls,
and set your table with rich food.{c}

Then comes the sound of a roar.
He thunders with his majestic voice.
Once his voice is heard, he holds back none of the lightning.{a}
